  "response": "## The scan\n\n`crimes scan test --format json` returns 82 findings; 26 are `weak_test_signal` (\"Test That Proves Nothing\") — 12 with claim `no_assertions`, 14 with `weak_assertion_matchers`.\n\nExactly one of them names an esbuild plugin, which matches the reported regression:\n\n**`crime_00029` — `test/index.test.ts:537-548`**, evidence verbatim:\n\n```\n\"test: \\\"native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly\\\"\",\n\"0 expect/assert calls\",\n\"lines 537-548\"\n```\n\nsummary: `Test \"native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly\" contains no expect/assert calls. A test that only runs setup gives agents false confidence.`\n\n## Why this one first\n\nThe test body is literally a bare `await run(...)` with no trailing assertion:\n\n```ts\ntest('native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly', async () => {\n  await run(getTestName(), {\n    'input.tsx': `export * from './hi.node'`,\n    'hi.node.js': `export const hi = 'hi'`,\n  }, { entry: ['input.tsx'] })\n})\n```\n\n`run()` (`test/utils.ts:56`) only throws on a non-zero exit code. So the test passes for exactly one reason: **the build didn't crash.** Every behavior the plugin actually implements is unchecked.\n\nThe plugin's whole job here (`src/esbuild/native-node-module.ts`) is the `.node(.js)` discrimination — `onResolve` puts a path in the `node-file` namespace *only* `if (resolvedId.endsWith('.node'))`, otherwise it returns `{ path: resolvedId }` and lets esbuild bundle it normally. Break that branch and `hi.node.js` gets routed through the `node-file` loader, emitted as a copied file asset with a `require(path)` shim instead of inlined source. The build still exits 0. The test still passes. That is the regression shape the user is describing.\n\n## The assertion I'd add\n\nCapture the result and assert the *contents* of `dist/input.js`, plus the file list:\n\n```ts\ntest('native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly', async () => {\n  const { output, outFiles } = await run(getTestName(), {\n    'input.tsx': `export * from './hi.node'`,\n    'hi.node.js': `export const hi = 'hi'`,\n  }, { entry: ['input.tsx'] })\n\n  // hi.node.js is NOT a native addon — it must be inlined, not routed\n  // through the node-file namespace.\n  expect(output).toContain(`hi = \"hi\"`)\n  expect(output).not.toContain('node-file')\n  expect(output).not.toMatch(/require\\(path\\)/)\n\n  // and no .node asset should be copied into dist\n  expect(outFiles).toEqual(['input.js'])\n})\n```\n\nThe load-bearing line is `expect(output).toContain('hi = \"hi\"')` — it proves the module was *bundled*, not file-copied, which is precisely the `.node` vs `.node.js` distinction the test name claims to protect. `outFiles` is the cheap companion check: a misrouted file shows up as an extra hashed asset.\n\nPattern precedent is already in the repo — `test/shims.test.ts:49` does `expect(outFiles).toStrictEqual(['index.mjs'])` then asserts exact file content.\n\n## Two notes on the rest of the list\n\n- **`crime_00030` and `crime_00031` (`test/shims.test.ts`) are false positives.** Both report `\"0 expect/assert calls\"`, but those tests do assert — `expect(outFiles).toStrictEqual(['index.mjs'])` and `expect(await getFileContent('dist/index.mjs')).toStrictEqual(indexMjsContent)`. The tell is the finding's own `\"lines 5-5\"`: the detector collapsed the range because the callback is `async ({ expect, task })` rather than `async ()`, so it never walked the body. Skip these; don't \"fix\" tests that are already strict.\n- The remaining `no_assertions` hits (`test/graphql.test.ts`, `test/example.test.ts`, `test/experimental-dts.test.ts`, `test/tsconfig.test.ts:4-17`) are the same smoke-test-only shape and worth a follow-up pass, but none of them covers an esbuild plugin, so they're not your regression.",
